From bdad2c57ebee8c17b80f9798d6f6514354a747a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 11:05:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] agentHost: make chat/turnStarted reducer idempotent

The chat subscription keeps an optimistic overlay: every action the client
dispatches is applied locally, held in the pending list, and replayed on top
of the confirmed state each time a confirmed action arrives. A pending action
is only retired when the server echoes it back with the client's own
clientSeq, and some paths never echo, so a turn start can stay pending for
the whole turn.

That was harmless for most cases, but the ChatTurnStarted reducer case
unconditionally rebuilt activeTurn with an empty responseParts array. Once
the start was confirmed by another path, every replay wiped the streamed
content again, so the response looked blank for as long as the start stayed
pending. This is the mechanism behind microsoft/vscode#332087.

ChatTurnStarted now returns the state unchanged when its turnId is already
the active turn, or already appears in the completed turns (the turn ended
and was confirmed through a snapshot). A replayed start therefore never
resets a turn in progress and never resurrects a finished turn as an empty
active one. Restarting a finished turn remains ChatTurnResume's job.

The other client-dispatchable chat cases were audited and are already
idempotent: they either guard on a status the first application leaves
behind (tool call transitions, turn cancel and resume, input completion,
pending message removal) or assign the same value on every application
(content, draft, working directories, pending message set, reorder, input
answers). ChatTruncated is already idempotent as well; a test locks that in.

The reducer is synced from the agent-host-protocol repo, so the same change
is made there on a matching branch with fixtures. Tests cover the replay at
the reducer level and the stranded turn start at the subscription level.
